WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE ACCUSED

RESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED:

I. CIVIL VS CRIMINAL DISTINCTION

The dispute between the bank and the applicant is essentially civil in nature. It relates to loan repayment and not to any criminal offence. The Supreme Court has consistently held that civil disputes should not be converted into criminal cases.

II. NO DISHONEST INTENTION AT INCEPTION

The prosecution has failed to establish dishonest intention at the time of obtaining the loan. The applicant genuinely intended to repay and had a viable business plan. The subsequent financial setbacks do not establish mens rea at inception.

III. DOCUMENTS WERE GENUINE

The allegation of forged documents is based on misinterpretation. The documents submitted were genuine business documents. The bank's own due process was followed at the time of sanction.

IV. FUND DIVERSION WAS BUSINESS-RELATED

The diversion of funds was due to genuine business exigencies and not with dishonest intention. The funds were used for legitimate business purposes though not exactly as stated in the loan application.

V. REPAYMENT EFFORTS MADE

The applicant made several repayment attempts, which demonstrate his bona fides. The inability to repay in full was due to business losses and not intentional default.

VI. ALTERNATIVE REMEDIES AVAILABLE

Civil remedies are available to the bank. The bank can proceed under the Securitisation Act for recovery of dues. Criminal proceedings are not warranted.

VII. BURDEN OF PROOF ON PROSECUTION

The prosecution has failed to prove the ingredients of Sections 420, 406, and 409 beyond reasonable doubt. The evidence is circumstantial and does not establish criminal intent.

VIII. PRECEDENTS ON ECONOMIC OFFENCES

The Supreme Court has held that in economic offences, the court must be cautious before framing charges. The distinction between civil breach and criminal offence must be maintained.

PRAYER

It is therefore prayed that the applicant be discharged/acquitted as the case may be.

